## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a Kubernetes-native control plane for GPU-backed ML workloads, balancing quotas, preemption, and data access while preserving isolation and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Kubernetes Operators and CRDs
- GPU scheduling with taints, tolerations, and resource requests
- Priority, preemption and multi-tenant quotas
- Data locality and streaming of dataset shards
- Observability, auditing, and lifecycle finalizers

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo reconciler loop for GpuTrainJob
while (true) {
  const jobs = listGpuTrainJobs();
  for (const j of jobs) {
    if (!quotaOk(j)) continue;
    if (!podExists(j)) {
      createGpuPod(j); // with nodeSelector for j.gpuType and resources
    }
    updateStatus(j, {phase: "Running"});
  }
  await sleep(1000);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate preemption scenarios in tests?
- How would you handle mixed GPU architectures and driver versions in the same cluster?

## Why This Is Asked
Isolating ephemeral testing environments in Kubernetes is common in multi-tenant SaaS. This question probes CRD design, lifecycle management, resource isolation, and observability under churn.

## Key Concepts
- Kubernetes CRD and controller pattern
- Namespace isolation, ResourceQuota, LimitRange
- NetworkPolicy for tenant isolation
- Finalizers, TTL-based cleanup
- Observability: metrics, events, tracing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-reconcile flow
function reconcile(ee){
  if(!namespaceExists(ee.spec.name)) createNamespace(ee.spec.name);
  applyQuota(ee.spec.quotas, ee.spec.name);
  applyPolicy(ee.spec.networkPolicy, ee.spec.name);
  addFinalizer(ee);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How handle TTL expiry with running pods? 
- How ensure idempotent reconciliations during crashes?
